# STATE — what is true right now

> **This file outranks everything else on the question "is X still open?"**
> A closed row here beats any older mention in tasks.md, a past brief, or a wiki note.
>
> **Update it the same turn reality changes.** If {{USER_NAME}} mentions in passing
> that something is done, the row closes now — not at end of day. This rule exists
> because the alternative was re-nagging them about work they had already finished.
>
> Every row carries a **Verified** date: the last time evidence actually confirmed it.
>
> 🔴 **No pointer, no row.** A row claiming a specific person said, sent, or promised a specific
> thing must carry its evidence — the message, the date, or where to find it — at the moment it is
> written. An invented row here does not stay a small error: everything downstream treats this file
> as ground truth and re-broadcasts it.
>
> **Two more rules, both paid for in real mistakes:**
> 1. **A flag coming back a second time is a cue to re-examine the inference under it, not to raise
>    it louder.** A repeated flag is usually one wrong assumption on a loop, not rising urgency.
> 2. **When a row closes, record WHY it closed, not just that it did.** A bare status flip lets a
>    later pass re-derive the same wrong conclusion from the same gap.
>
> **Size rules — this file is read at every session start, so its length taxes every single run:**
> - A row is current status only, not the story of how you learned it. Budget about three lines;
>   past that the narrative moves to the daily log or a note, and the row keeps a pointer.
> - **A closed or resolved section does not live here.** Move it to `memory/_archive/` the turn it
>   closes — and **search that archive before concluding something never happened.**
> - **Past ~300 lines, evict before you add.** The session-start hook fits this file to a
>   budget by section priority — fuses survive, lower sections get dropped with a notice —
>   so a bloated file means the agent wakes on a clipped view of reality, starting with
>   whatever mattered least.

## ACTIVE FIVE (hard cap)

> Five live bets. **You cannot add a sixth without killing one.** Every row is
> ACT / SCHEDULED-with-a-date / KILL. Anything untouched for two weeks dies by
> default — the agent has to justify keeping it, not the user justify dropping it.
>
> This replaced an earlier "never idle, always pick up the next task" instruction.
> That sounds productive and is actually a drift generator: it produced piles of
> work nobody asked for. Capacity is not the constraint; attention is.

